## The glossary: terms (the name, the stems it is found by in a text — .stems, the footnote, more).
## Stems: «=» — the whole word, «*» — any ending inside; separated by commas.

term-cathode = Cathode
    .stems = cathode
term-cathode-text = The electrode joined to the “minus” of the power source. Positive ions (cations) move to it and take electrons: reduction happens here — hydrogen forms or a metal settles.
term-anode = Anode
    .stems = anode
term-anode-text = The electrode joined to the “plus” of the power source. Here a substance gives up electrons (is oxidised): chlorine or oxygen forms — or a metal anode itself dissolves.
term-electrode = Electrode
    .stems = electrode
term-electrode-text = A conductor (a plate, a rod) through which current enters or leaves a liquid. Electrodes are inert (graphite) or soluble (a metal that reacts itself).
term-electrolysis = Electrolysis
    .stems = electrolys
term-electrolysis-text = Breaking a substance down with an electric current: new substances (gases, metals) form at the electrodes. How much forms is given by Faraday’s law.
term-electrolyte = Electrolyte
    .stems = electrolyte
term-electrolyte-text = A substance whose solution or melt conducts current because it holds free ions: salts, acids, alkalis. Sugar and alcohol are not electrolytes.
term-ion = Ion
    .stems = ion, cation, anion
term-ion-text = An atom or a group of atoms with an electric charge: positive (cations, Na⁺, Cu²⁺) and negative (anions, Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻). In a salt solution ions move freely and carry the current.
term-electrode-potential = Electrode potential
    .stems = potential
term-electrode-potential-text = A number in volts (E°) showing how easily a substance gives up or takes electrons. The lower a metal’s potential, the more easily it is oxidised: iron (−0.44 V) more easily than copper (+0.34 V).
term-voltage = Voltage
    .stems = voltage
term-voltage-text = What “pushes” charges round a circuit; measured in volts (V). The higher the voltage at the same resistance, the larger the current.
term-current = Electric current
    .stems = current
term-current-text = How much charge passes through a conductor per second; measured in amperes (A). Current is an orderly flow of charged particles: electrons in a metal, ions in a solution.
term-resistance = Resistance
    .stems = resistance
term-resistance-text = How strongly a conductor “resists” the current; measured in ohms (Ω). By Ohm’s law I = U/R.
term-specific-heat = Specific heat capacity
    .stems = specific heat, heat capacity
term-specific-heat-text = How much heat it takes to warm 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C. Water’s is very large — ≈ 4200 J/(kg·°C), so water warms up and cools down slowly.
term-latent-heat = Latent heat
    .stems = latent heat
term-latent-heat-text = The heat a substance takes in while it melts or boils (and gives back while it sets or condenses) — its temperature stays the same meanwhile. Hence the flat “shelf” on the graph.
term-thermal-equilibrium = Thermal equilibrium
    .stems = thermal equilibrium, heat balance
term-thermal-equilibrium-text = The state when touching bodies have the same temperature and heat no longer flows. As much heat as the hot body gave, the cold one took (the heat-balance equation).
term-efficiency = Efficiency
    .stems = efficiency
term-efficiency-text = How much of the energy spent did the useful job. η = Q/E; always below 100 %.
term-conduction = Thermal conduction
    .stems = thermal conduct, conducts heat
term-conduction-text = Heat passing from the hotter part of a body to the colder one without the substance moving. Metals conduct heat well, air and porcelain poorly.
term-radiation = Thermal radiation
    .stems = radiat
term-radiation-text = Heat carried by light — visible and invisible infrared. A red-hot body loses most of its heat this way; this is how the Sun warms the Earth.
term-evaporation = Evaporation
    .stems = evaporat
term-evaporation-text = A liquid turning into vapour from its surface — at any temperature, not only when boiling. The warmer and the drier the air, the faster.
term-boiling = Boiling
    .stems = boil
term-boiling-text = Vapour forming throughout the liquid: bubbles of vapour appear inside. It happens at the boiling point, which depends on the pressure and on what is dissolved.
term-crystallisation = Crystallisation
    .stems = crystallis, crystalliz
term-crystallisation-text = A liquid turning solid as its particles line up into a crystal lattice; also crystals coming out of a saturated solution.
term-eutectic = Eutectic
    .stems = eutectic
term-eutectic-text = The lowest temperature at which a solution can still be liquid: −21.1 °C for salt water. Below it everything sets — both water and salt.
term-solution = Solution
    .stems = =solution, =solutions
term-solution-text = An even mixture: one substance (the solute) spread evenly among the particles of another (the solvent, most often water). The dissolved particles cannot be seen even under a microscope.
term-solubility = Solubility
    .stems = solubil
term-solubility-text = How much of a substance can dissolve in 100 g of water at a given temperature. Any more falls out as crystals. Sugar’s solubility grows strongly with heat, salt’s hardly at all.
term-heat-of-solution = Enthalpy of solution
    .stems = heat of solution, enthalpy of solution
term-heat-of-solution-text = The heat a substance takes in (+) or gives off (−) as it dissolves: Q = n·ΔH. Ammonium chloride (+14.8 kJ/mol) cools the water, sodium hydroxide (−44.5 kJ/mol) heats it. When the substance crystallises, the heat comes back.
term-supersaturated = Supersaturated solution
    .stems = supersaturat
term-supersaturated-text = A solution holding more dissolved substance than its solubility allows at that temperature. It happens when a hot saturated solution is cooled carefully: the crystals have nothing to start growing on. A seed crystal is enough — and the excess quickly crystallises. All solutions supersaturate a little, but substances with a complex lattice and a thick solution (sodium acetate, sugar) do so strongly, copper sulfate or table salt only slightly.
term-mass-fraction = Mass fraction
    .stems = mass fraction
term-mass-fraction-text = What part of the mass of a mixture or solution a substance makes up: w = m(substance) / m(solution). In 50 g of solution with 5 g of salt w = 5/50 = 0.1, that is 10 %. Divide by the mass of the whole solution, not only the water.
term-saturated = Saturated solution
    .stems = saturat
term-saturated-text = A solution holding as much of a substance as can dissolve at that temperature. Anything added beyond it no longer dissolves.
term-precipitate = Precipitate
    .stems = precipitat, sludge
term-precipitate-text = An insoluble substance formed in a solution by a reaction; it falls to the bottom or clouds the liquid.
term-distillation = Distillation
    .stems = distil
term-distillation-text = Separating a mixture of liquids by boiling: the lower-boiling substance (alcohol) turns to vapour first and is collected by cooling the vapour.
term-oxidation = Oxidation
    .stems = oxidi, oxidat
term-oxidation-text = A reaction in which a substance gives up electrons. Iron rusting and a metal anode dissolving are oxidation; the opposite is reduction.
term-stearic-acid = Stearic acid
    .stems = stearic
term-stearic-acid-text = A fatty acid, C₁₈H₃₆O₂ — a white waxy substance used for candles and soap. It melts at 69.3 °C and gives off ≈ 215 kJ/kg of latent heat as it sets — which is why it is used for a cooling graph with a flat “shelf”.
term-airship = Airship
    .stems = airship, zeppelin nt
term-airship-text = A steerable aircraft lighter than air: its envelope is filled with a gas lighter than air, and the buoyant force holds it up; engines and rudders take it where it should go. Airships today, like the Zeppelin NT, are filled with non-flammable helium; they usually fly 300 m up.
term-planck-constant = The Planck constant
    .stems = planck constant
term-planck-constant-text = One of the basic constants of nature, h = 6.62607015·10⁻³⁴ J·s. Max Planck brought it in in 1900: the energy of a portion of light is h times its frequency. Since 2019 its value is fixed exactly — and the kilogram is defined through it.
term-kibble-balance = Kibble balance
    .stems = kibble balance
term-kibble-balance-text = Scales where an electromagnet takes the place of the weight: the body’s weight is balanced by the force of a magnet on a coil carrying a current, and from the current and the voltage, with the Planck constant known, the mass is worked out. Bryan Kibble proposed the principle in 1975; since 2019 such balances realise the kilogram.
term-pale-blue-dot = Pale Blue Dot
    .stems = pale blue dot
term-pale-blue-dot-text = The picture of the Earth that the Voyager 1 probe took on 14 February 1990 from over 6 billion kilometres — on the astronomer Carl Sagan’s idea. The Earth fills 0.12 of a pixel in it: a tiny dot in a beam of scattered sunlight.
term-hindenburg = The Hindenburg
    .stems = hindenburg
term-hindenburg-text = A passenger airship filled with hydrogen. On 6 May 1937, landing at Lakehurst (USA), it caught fire and burned in about half a minute.
term-beauplan = Guillaume Le Vasseur de Beauplan
    .stems = beauplan
term-beauplan-text = A French-Polish cartographer, engineer and architect (c. 1600 – 1673). The author of the «General Map of Ukraine» (1648) and of the book «Description d’Ukranie» (1660).
term-beauplan-map = Beauplan’s General Map of Ukraine
    .stems = general map of
term-beauplan-map-text = Guillaume de Beauplan’s map of 1648 — the «General Map of the Wild Fields, in common speech Ukraine» — with its borders drawn in detail. Later Willem Hondius engraved two of Beauplan’s maps — at scales of 1 : 452,000 and 1 : 1,800,000.
term-led-zeppelin = Led Zeppelin
    .stems = led zeppelin
term-led-zeppelin-text = A rock band. The cover of its first album (1969) is George Hardie’s drawing of Sam Shere’s photograph of the burning Hindenburg, taken on 6 May 1937. The name itself is a joke: “it would go over like a lead balloon”.
term-density = Density
    .stems = density
term-density-text = The mass of a unit volume of a substance: ρ = m/V. A body denser than the liquid sinks, a less dense one floats.
term-buoyancy = Buoyant force
    .stems = buoyan
term-buoyancy-text = The force with which a liquid pushes a submerged body up; it equals the weight of the liquid the body displaces: F_A = ρ·g·V.
term-resistor = Resistor
    .stems = resistor
term-resistor-text = A circuit part with a fixed resistance: it limits the current. Its resistance is marked by coloured bands or figures.
term-resistor-more = The simplest resistor is a wire of an alloy with a high resistivity (nichrome, constantan) wound on a ceramic core; in modern electronics — a thin film of carbon or metal on a ceramic cylinder. Resistance is measured in ohms (Ω). Current goes through a resistor, as through any conductor, by Ohm’s law: I = U / R. The current warms it (Joule–Lenz), so each is also rated for the most power it can take. The coloured bands are read left to right: the first digits of the resistance, then a multiplier and the tolerance.
term-rheostat = Rheostat
    .stems = rheostat
term-rheostat-text = A resistor whose resistance can be changed smoothly with a slider — to adjust the current in a circuit.
term-rheostat-more = A school slide rheostat is a wire wound on a ceramic tube; a slider moves over the turns and puts more or fewer of them into the circuit. The longer the part of the wire in use, the higher the resistance and the smaller the current. A rheostat sets a lamp’s brightness or a motor’s speed; it is connected in series with the device.
term-ammeter = Ammeter
    .stems = ammeter
term-ammeter-text = An instrument that measures current. It is connected in series — so that all the current being measured goes through it.
term-ammeter-more = An ammeter has a very small resistance of its own, so as to barely change the current; that is why it is never connected straight to a source with nothing else — the current would be huge and spoil it. Its scale has a largest value (the range) and a division — which sets how precisely it can be read. Its terminals are marked “+” and “−”: “+” goes to the source’s plus. The school instruments of this lab: a 2 A range, 0.05 A per division (an estimate).
term-voltmeter = Voltmeter
    .stems = voltmeter
term-voltmeter-text = An instrument that measures voltage. It is connected in parallel with the part of the circuit whose voltage is measured.
term-voltmeter-more = A voltmeter has a very large resistance of its own, so a tiny current goes through it and it barely changes the circuit. Its terminals go to the two ends of the part — a resistor, for example; “+” nearer the source’s plus. Like an ammeter, its scale has a range and a division. The school instruments of this lab: a 6 V range, 0.2 V per division (an estimate).
term-power-source = Power source
    .stems = power source, power supply
term-power-source-text = A device that keeps a voltage between its terminals and makes charges move round a circuit: a battery, an accumulator, a power supply.
term-power-source-more = Inside a source, outside forces — chemical in a battery, electric in a mains power supply — separate charges: “plus” at one terminal, “minus” at the other. Close the circuit and the charges go from terminal to terminal through the devices. A lab power supply gives a safe low voltage set with a knob. A source also has a resistance of its own (internal), so under load the voltage at its terminals is a little lower.
term-switch = Switch (key)
    .stems = switch
term-switch-text = A part that closes or opens a circuit. Closed — the current flows; open — there is no current.
term-switch-more = In school circuits the key is a small metal lever on an insulating base: pressed down, it joins two terminals. A circuit is built with the key open, and it is closed only once everything is checked — a safety rule. The light switch in a room is the same key in a case.
term-multimeter = Multimeter
    .stems = multimeter
term-multimeter-text = An “all-in-one” instrument: a dial chooses what to measure — voltage, current or resistance.
term-multimeter-more = A digital multimeter set to “Ω” passes a small known current through the conductor itself and divides the voltage across it by that current — it shows the resistance R = U / I. The range is chosen with room to spare: on “200 Ω” it reads up to 200 Ω in hundredths. Resistance is measured only in a circuit with no power on: another source would spoil the reading.
term-diode = Diode
    .stems = diode
term-diode-text = A part that lets current through in one direction only — like a valve for current.
term-diode-more = A modern diode is a crystal of a semiconductor (silicon) whose two parts conduct differently: a layer forms at the boundary that lets charges through one way only. Connected “forward”, a diode has a small resistance; “in reverse”, a huge one. Diodes turn alternating current into direct (in chargers), and light-emitting diodes also glow when current goes through them.
term-transistor = Transistor
    .stems = transistor
term-transistor-text = A semiconductor part in which a small current or voltage controls a large current: an amplifier and a switch.
term-transistor-more = A transistor has three leads: the main current flows through two, and the third controls it — like a tap opened by a light touch. So a transistor amplifies weak signals (in headphones, radios) and switches current on and off billions of times a second. A modern processor is billions of tiny transistors on one silicon chip.
term-dynamometer = Dynamometer
    .stems = dynamometer, spring balance
term-dynamometer-text = An instrument that measures force: a spring that stretches the more, the larger the force, and a scale in newtons.
term-dynamometer-more = A dynamometer works by Hooke’s law: the spring’s extension is proportional to the force, so the scale can be marked evenly. Hanging a body on it measures its weight; pulling a block steadily — the friction. Before measuring the pointer is set to zero, and the scale is read straight on. The school dynamometer of this lab: up to 5 N, 0.1 N per division.
term-measuring-cylinder = Measuring cylinder
    .stems = measuring cylinder, graduated cylinder
term-measuring-cylinder-text = A glass vessel with marks for measuring the volume of a liquid — and of a body put into it.
term-measuring-cylinder-more = The level is read at the bottom of the meniscus (the curved water surface), with the eyes at its height. The volume of an odd-shaped body is the difference of the levels before and after: V = V₂ − V₁. Before measuring, find the division: the difference of two labelled marks over the number of divisions between them. A cylinder is never heated.
term-calorimeter = Calorimeter
    .stems = calorimeter
term-calorimeter-text = A vessel that hardly exchanges heat with the air — for heat-balance experiments.
term-calorimeter-more = A school calorimeter is two metal cups one inside the other with an air gap and an insulating stand: air conducts heat poorly, so what is inside keeps its temperature for long. Thanks to that, the heat given by one body almost all goes to another, and the heat balance gives a specific heat or a latent heat.
term-stopwatch = Stopwatch
    .stems = stopwatch
term-stopwatch-text = An instrument for measuring time intervals: started at the beginning of a motion, stopped at its end.
term-stopwatch-more = An electronic stopwatch (in a phone too) shows hundredths of a second, but a person starts and stops it about 0.1–0.2 s late. So short intervals are timed over many repeats — say, 20 swings of a pendulum — and divided by their number: the hand’s error is divided too.
term-stand = Lab stand
    .stems = lab stand, retort stand
term-stand-text = A base with an upright rod to which clamps, rings and bosses are fixed — to hold apparatus at the right height.
term-stand-more = A heavy base keeps the stand from tipping over, and a boss lets a clamp or a ring be fixed at any height. A spring and a pendulum hang from it; a groove, a test tube over a spirit lamp or the end of a sloping board are clamped to it.
term-lever-balance = Lever balance
    .stems = lever balance, beam balance
term-lever-balance-text = A balance with a beam on an axis and two pans: a body is balanced with a set of weights.
term-lever-balance-more = It is an equal-armed lever: when the masses on the pans are equal, the beam is level. The body goes on the left pan, the weights with tweezers on the right, from larger to smaller; the body’s mass is the sum of the weights. A balance compares masses, so it would show the same on the Moon. The smallest weight of the set sets the precision.
term-thermometer = Thermometer
    .stems = thermometer
term-thermometer-text = An instrument that measures temperature.
term-thermometer-more = A liquid thermometer is a thin glass tube with a liquid (alcohol, mercury in the past) that expands when warmed and climbs the scale. To show a body’s temperature, the thermometer must come to the same temperature — so it is held in the water until the column stops. Alcohol thermometers work in hard frost too: alcohol freezes only at −114 °C.
term-test-tube = Test tube
    .stems = test tube
term-test-tube-text = A narrow glass tube closed at one end — for experiments with small amounts of substances.
term-test-tube-more = Test tubes are made of heat-resistant (borosilicate) glass, so they can be heated over a flame, held in a holder with the mouth turned away from everyone. Stoppered, a tube becomes a sealed body of fixed volume — in the floating experiment sand is poured in to change its mass.
term-rhind-papyrus = The Rhind papyrus
    .stems = rhind papyrus
term-rhind-papyrus-text = An Egyptian handbook of mathematics, copied about 1550 BC by the scribe Ahmes from an older text. Named after Alexander Henry Rhind, who bought it in Luxor in 1858; in the British Museum since 1865.
term-palette = Palette
    .stems = palette
term-palette-text = A transparent sheet (or paper) ruled in equal squares — to find the area of a figure of irregular shape.
term-palette-more = The palette is laid on the figure and the squares are counted: N whole ones inside and n cut by the edge; the cut ones are taken by half, so the area S ≈ (N + n/2)·s₀, where s₀ is one square’s area. The smaller the squares, the more accurate, but the longer to count.
